# Known divergences, all by design. plpgsql_check's suite feeds multigres exactly
# the PL/pgSQL patterns the connection pooler refuses, so the rejections below are
# the intended behavior a pooled user sees -- recorded here rather than hidden.
#
# 1. LOAD is rejected (loading shared libraries through the pooler is not
#    permitted; see go/services/multigateway/planner/unsafe_stmt.go). The library
#    is preloaded cluster-wide via shared_preload_libraries (PreloadLibraries on
#    the plpgsql_check spec in extensions.go), so the LOAD is a semantic no-op and
#    only the extra ERROR line differs.
#
# 2. multigateway parses every PL/pgSQL body at CREATE for safety analysis and
#    rejects (a) bodies it cannot parse -- the suite deliberately crafts functions
#    with errors PostgreSQL only reports at run time (bad RAISE argument counts,
#    CONTINUE label misuse, syntax errors) -- and (b) bodies containing a dynamic
#    EXECUTE, whose runtime-built statement text cannot be vetted for unsafe
#    session-state changes. stock PG defers all of these: it creates the function
#    and the error surfaces later, whereas multigres rejects the CREATE up front.
#
# 3. Each rejected `create or replace f1` leaves no (or a stale) f1, so the test's
#    later `select f1()` / `plpgsql_check_function('f1()')` / `drop function f1()`
#    report "does not exist". That cascade is a consequence of the rejections
#    above, not a separate divergence.
--- a
+++ b
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 load 'plpgsql';
+ERROR: LOAD is not supported: loading shared libraries is not permitted through the connection pooler
 create extension if not exists plpgsql_check;
 set client_min_messages to notice;
 set plpgsql_check.regress_test_mode = true;
@@ -503,13 +504,14 @@
